      "text": "We want to find the value of $\\cos 150^\\circ$. \n\nSince the angle is greater than 90 degrees and less than 180 degrees, the terminal side of the 150-degree angle lies in the second quadrant. \n\nIn the second quadrant, the cosine of an angle is negative. Therefore, $\\cos 150^\\circ$ is negative. \n\nUsing the reference angle of 30 degrees, we can write \n\n$$\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\cos(180^\\circ - 150^\\circ) = -\\cos 30^\\circ$$\n\nFrom trigonometric identities, we know that $\\cos 30^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. Thus, \n\n$$- \\cos 30^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$$\nTherefore, $\\cos 150^\\circ = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. We can write our final answer as $\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}$. \n\nThe answer is: -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}",
